## Part B — Future phases (kept deliberately small)

Four phases. Each one should leave you with something that *works end-to-end*, so if you run out of time after any phase, you still have a demoable product.

### Phase 1 — Real data backbone (Supabase: Auth + Postgres + Storage + RLS)
Replace every mock function in `services.ts`/`admin-services.ts` with real Supabase calls. Real signup/login. Real persisted schemes, applications, documents, audit logs. The UI barely changes — you're swapping the engine under the hood. **This is the next phase, detailed in full in Part E below.**

### Phase 2 — The real AI workforce (LangGraph on Render, calling Groq)
Build the actual multi-agent orchestrator in Python (LangGraph + LangChain) as a standalone service hosted on Render. It exposes one endpoint (`POST /run`) that takes a citizen query + profile and streams back agent-by-agent events (`agent_started`, `agent_message`, `agent_done`, `handoff`) that map directly onto the `AgentEvent` type you already defined. A Supabase Edge Function acts as the secure proxy between your frontend and Render (so your Groq key and Render URL never touch the browser), and either relays the stream or writes events into `agent_events` which the frontend subscribes to via **Supabase Realtime**. The `/assistant` page's `setTimeout` choreography gets replaced with real streamed state — visually it can look almost identical, which is good: you already validated that UX with judges/mentors mentally, now it's just true.

### Phase 3 — Document intelligence + live orchestration polish
Real document upload to Supabase Storage, real extraction (Groq vision model or an OCR step) feeding into `checkEligibility`, and tightening the Realtime pipe so `/admin/agents` shows a genuinely live multi-citizen agent feed — this is your single best "technical depth" demo moment for judges, so it's worth a dedicated phase rather than bolting it onto Phase 2 under time pressure.

### Phase 4 — Pitch hardening & deployment
Seed script for the exact demo persona (Rahul Sharma) so every demo run is reproducible, `/admin/analytics` wired to real aggregate queries instead of `MOCK_ADMIN_METRICS`, an audited pass over every empty/loading/error state, a pre-recorded backup demo video (Groq/Render can hiccup live — never bet a final on live network calls alone), and rehearsal of the judge Q&A story (see Part C).

