## Part D — LLM choice: Groq models, checked as of today (Sept 2026)

You asked me to check current Groq models rather than assume from memory — good instinct, this catalog moves fast. Key facts as of now:

- Groq **deprecated `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` and `llama-3.1-8b-instant`** — Groq's own docs list an Aug 16, 2026 shutdown for these, so **do not build on them**, even though most tutorials online still reference them.
- Current recommended replacements: **`openai/gpt-oss-120b`** (strong reasoning + tool calling, replaces 70B-class usage) and **`openai/gpt-oss-20b`** (fast/cheap, replaces 8B-class usage).
- Also available: `meta-llama/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct` (vision-capable), `qwen/qwen3.6-27b` (multimodal, marked preview — fine for a hackathon demo, not for anything you'd call production), and `compound-beta` / `compound-beta-mini` — Groq's own hybrid models with **built-in web search and code execution tools**, free tier, which could save you from building a custom retrieval tool for the Scheme Agent if you want a shortcut.
- **Free tier:** no credit card required, full model catalog, rate-limited (roughly 30 requests/minute per model, org-wide — multiple API keys don't raise this). That's genuinely workable for a live hackathon demo with a handful of judges hitting it, but it means you should **not** hammer it during dev/testing with the same key you'll demo with, and you should implement basic retry/backoff (Groq returns `429` with a `retry-after` header).

**Recommendation for Sahayak's agent roles:**
| Agent | Model | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Citizen Agent (intent understanding) | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | Fast, cheap, task is simple extraction/classification |
| Scheme Agent (retrieval + ranking) | `openai/gpt-oss-120b` or `compound-beta` | Needs reasoning over multiple candidates; `compound-beta` if you want free built-in search |
| Eligibility Agent (rule evaluation + explanation) | `openai/gpt-oss-120b` | This is your "evidence-backed" differentiator — needs the stronger model to write correct, well-justified explanations |
| Document Agent (field extraction) | `qwen/qwen3.6-27b` (vision) or an OCR library + `openai/gpt-oss-20b` for structuring | Vision model if you want true image understanding; classic OCR (Tesseract) + LLM structuring is the safer fallback if the vision model proves flaky mid-hackathon |
| Application Agent (draft assembly) | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | Mostly templating verified data, doesn't need heavy reasoning |

Store the model ID as an environment variable per agent, not hardcoded — Groq's catalog will keep shifting and you don't want a repeat of the 70B deprecation mid-hackathon.

