## Part E — In-depth Phase 1 implementation plan

This is the plan to hand to your coding agent. Phase 1's job: **make the app real, without changing what it looks like.** Every route should still work exactly as it does today, but backed by an actual database and actual auth instead of mock arrays and `localStorage`.

### E.1 Goals & definition of done
- A real Supabase project exists with a complete schema matching the domain types already defined in the codebase.
- Signup/login uses real Supabase Auth (email + password is enough; don't over-build this).
- Route guards (`beforeLoad` checks currently reading `localStorage.getItem("sahayak_auth")`) check a real Supabase session instead.
- Every function currently exported from `src/lib/services.ts` and `src/lib/admin-services.ts` reads/writes real Supabase tables, with the **same function signatures** so route components need minimal changes.
- Row Level Security is enabled and correct: a citizen can only ever see their own rows; an admin role can see everything.
- A seed script reliably recreates the Rahul Sharma demo scenario (profile, 5 schemes, one in-progress application with a missing document) so every demo run starts from the same known-good state.
- **Phase 1 explicitly does not include real LLM calls.** `understandCitizenNeed`, `checkEligibility` etc. can keep their current mock/keyword logic for now — only their *data persistence* becomes real (e.g., a `checkEligibility` call should write a real row to `applications`/`agent_events` even though the eligibility logic itself is still simple). This keeps Phase 1 scoped and testable independent of Phase 2's Python/Groq work landing.

### E.2 Architecture for this phase
```
React (TanStack Start, Vite)
   │  @supabase/supabase-js  (anon key, browser-safe)
   ▼
Supabase
   ├── Auth            → citizens table auto-linked via auth.users trigger
   ├── Postgres         → schemes, eligibility_rules, document_requirements,
   │                       applications, application_documents, documents,
   │                       agent_runs, agent_events, audit_logs,
   │                       consent_records, notifications
   ├── Storage           → 'documents' bucket, RLS-scoped per citizen
   ├── Row Level Security → citizen isolation + admin role bypass
   └── Edge Functions    → only where secrets/service-role are needed
                            (Phase 1 needs exactly one: seed-demo-data)
```
No Python, no Render, no Groq key touches this phase. That all arrives in Phase 2, when the Edge Function layer becomes the secure bridge to the Render-hosted LangGraph service.

### E.3 Supabase project setup
1. Create a new Supabase project (free tier is fine for a hackathon). Note the project URL and anon key.
2. Install the client: `npm i @supabase/supabase-js`.
3. Add env vars: `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`, `VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`. Since this is a TanStack Start app with SSR, confirm these are exposed client-side correctly (Vite requires the `VITE_` prefix for browser exposure — double check nothing server-only leaks the service role key into the client bundle).
4. Create `src/lib/supabase.ts` exporting a single client singleton, used everywhere instead of ad-hoc instantiation.
5. Enable email/password auth in the Supabase dashboard. Disable email confirmation for the hackathon demo (or you'll be stuck waiting on emails mid-pitch) — turn it back on later if this ever becomes real.

